this is a different interpretation in  the sense that it's based very closely  on Raj odd Kipling's book which he wrote  in 1894 so it is very much a story about  Mowgli's central journey his journey of  self-discovery he is a boy that is an  orphaned child that has grown up with  Wolves believes himself to be a wolf  until he reaches a moment in his  adolescence where he is becomes  painfully aware that the rest of the  journey is about him assimilating into  the world of man where he also finds  both good and bad so it's a very Mowgli  centric journey but it's also  historically placed and contextualized  in a India of the turn of the century  when the book was written the huge  challenge on this movie was really how  to make talking animals believable I  mean we've seen lots of talking animals  in movies and you can make them look  great and you can make them look as if  they're talking but actually can you  make them feel as if they're really  emotionally connecting with Mowgli and  that was the big challenge and so for  that we went to great lengths using  performance capture technology tom  hollander is incredible as tabacky the  hyena he is such a wicked sense of humor  Cate Blanchett's Python car to bring a  Python to life and to really connect  with that because they have such dead  eyes in real life and what cater  standards extrordinary she becomes this  Prophet character but there are many  many great performances from all of our  cars Rowan chant who plays Mowgli is a  real revelation and his story of  self-discovery of learning who he is in  both worlds is amazing you really go  through it with Mowgli and I think it  should be emotionally engaging and you  know at times it'll make you feel sorry  for him it'll make you feel angry on his  behalf you through his eyes will really  hopefully feel what he's going through  [Music]
 

